9. CORRELATION
==================================================

Memory operations triggered by orchestration should preserve:

- requestId
- executionId
- correlationId

Use existing IDs.

Do not generate competing IDs.

Event log entries should make it possible to answer:

"Which orchestration caused this memory operation?"

==================================================
10. CONTEXT ASSEMBLY
==================================================

Reuse Sprint 5A ContextIntegrationService.

Do NOT build a second context assembly engine.

The integration should map:

RetrievalResponse
        ↓
ContextIntegrationService
        ↓
Orchestrator Context

Respect:

- token budget
- max sections
- max records per section
- snippet length
- priority preservation
- secret sanitization

Never bypass the existing context integration service.

==================================================
11. MEMORY WRITE-BACK POLICY
==================================================

Do NOT automatically persist every user request or every response.

Create a typed policy boundary for future memory write-back.

Possible categories:

- NONE
- EXPLICIT
- EVENT_BASED
- SELECTIVE

Default to the safest existing behavior.

If write-back is implemented, it must be:

- authorization checked
- sanitized
- scoped
- bounded
- deterministic
- auditable

Do not create uncontrolled memory growth.

==================================================
12. MEMORY FROM EXECUTION RESULTS
==================================================

If execution results are considered for memory:

DO NOT persist raw execution output automatically.

Create a transformation/sanitization boundary.

Ensure:

- secrets removed
- credentials removed
- huge payloads bounded
- irrelevant metadata removed
- authorization preserved

If this requires a future summarization/LLM service, defer it.

No LLM integration in Sprint 8.

